messages:
maya: Hey team, just got a high-priority escalation from ACME. They're seeing streaming responses cut off after ~30m and then a bunch of 401s when their TS client reconnects. Customer says partial outputs are delivered and then the iterator stops. Billing spike noticed around the time of the reconnects :grimacing:

jordan: Got the thread. Any repro steps from them? Which SDK?

maya: TS SDK (v1.9.2). They use EventSource-style SSE wrapper we provide. Repro steps from support: open long stream, keep it idle for ~25-35 minutes, then their client auto-reconnects and sometimes receives duplicate tokens/partial events, or the server returns 401s on resume. They sent a snippet and logs. Dropped in #pastebin: `sse-log-1865`

sam: Can you paste the core logs here? I suspect token refresh flow + short-lived JWT is involved. Also which auth method (API key vs bearer refresh)?

maya: They're using short-lived bearer tokens minted by our private deploy with a custom TTL of 30m. Logs:``` 
ACME-client: connection open
ACME-client: received chunk {"choices":[{"delta":{"content":"Hello"}}]}
ACME-client: no data for 1800s
ACME-client: reconnect attempt 1
ACME-client: got 401 on /v1/streams/resume
ACME-client: iterator stopped, partial output length 246
```

eli: That aligns with token expiry race. But the curious bit is duplicates/partial outputs — suggests resume flow isn't deterministic when resume call returns non-200. Do they see any "ERR_STREAM_RESUME" or similar?

maya: They attached client-side trace: ``error: StreamResumeError(401) -> client retries with new token -> sometimes server replies with previous session's trailing chunks -> ouput duplicate or cut in half``

jordan: Ok. Two potential root causes: 1) our resume endpoint is returning 401 for expired tokens but then processing some buffered events before closing the WS/SSE connection (leads to partials), or 2) the TS SDK retry logic races with token refresh and replays a resume call for the same session id, causing duplicates.

sam: Quick mitigation for customer: set token TTL > 1hr or enable refresh-before-expiry (client-side) to avoid reconnects in the window. Also recommend bumping to SDK v1.9.5 where we added an opt-in heartbeat/keepalive on SSE (but note they are on 1.9.2).

maya: Customer asked for concrete repro to provide to eng. They gave a small reproducer that uses node fetch + EventSource polyfill and a refresh token that sleeps for 31m before refreshing. I'll paste the TS repro:```ts
// minimal repro
const es = new EventSource(urlWithShortToken);
es.onmessage = (m) => console.log('msg', m.data);
es.onerror = async () => {
  // simulate refresh + reconnect
  const refreshed = await fetch('/refresh');
  const newUrl = buildUrl(refreshed.token);
  // naive reconnect
  es.close();
  new EventSource(newUrl);
};
```

eli: Nice. I can reproduce locally if I set token TTL=30m and force idle for 31m. Repro steps captured, creating a unit test in the runtime suite. Escalating to eng-runtime. @jordan please add this to the deploy queue — seems like a resume edge.

jordan: Copy. I'll also check server logs for requests around the time window. If the resume handler is processing buffered output after 401, that's a server bug. If not, it's the SDK race.

oncall-bot: Pager triggered for eng-runtime oncall: jordan (priority: P2)

jordan: Preliminary findings: server returns 401 immediately when token invalid. But there's a separate background writer that may flush buffered tokens to the client connection before the HTTP response completes in corner cases (we saw a 206-like partial flush). Opened PR to add stricter ordering and ensure resume endpoint returns and teardown finishes before any buffered writes: https://git.internal/redwood/eng-runtime/pulls/4821 (WIP).

sam: For SDK short-term patch, I'll add a reconnect guard to avoid firing multiple resume attempts concurrently and to ignore late-arriving chunks when resuming if resume returned non-200. Prep patch in TS SDK PR: https://git.internal/redwood/devex/ts-sdks/pulls/207 (will backport to 1.9.x).

maya: Customer prefers a quick workaround they can apply now. Sam: can you give a minimal change they can apply client-side?

sam: Yes — naive workaround: on error, wait for token refresh to finish, then do a single reconnection attempt and send `?resume=false` to force a fresh session (avoids resume race). Example:```ts
// workaround
es.onerror = async () => {
  const token = await refresh();
  const fresh = buildUrl(token) + '&resume=false';
  es.close();
  new EventSource(fresh);
};
```

jordan: Timeline: server fix queued for next patch (target: patch release tomorrow). SDK patch targeted for next minor (backport hotfix if needed). I'll keep this thread updated.

maya: Thanks all — passing the workaround to ACME. I'll mark as escalated-fixed if they confirm it resolves. :thumbsup:
